  "content": {
    "id": "v2-7eec2e848-practice-slot-1-root",
    "type": "question",
    "label": null,
    "prompt": "For what positive integer values of $n$ is the function $f(x)=kx^n+c$, where $k$ and $c$ are nonzero real constants:",
    "layout": "grid",
    "columns": 2,
    "questionDiagrams": [],
    "children": [
      {
        "id": "v2-7eec2e848-practice-slot-1-s1-q-a",
        "type": "part",
        "label": "a",
        "prompt": "Even?",
        "layout": "list",
        "columns": null,
        "questionDiagrams": [],
        "children": [],
        "answer": {
          "short": "Even values: $n=2,4,6,8,\\ldots$.",
          "worked": "$$\\begin{align*}f(-x)&=k(-x)^n+c\\\\&=kx^n+c\\quad\\text{when }n\\text{ is even}\\\\&=f(x).\\end{align*}$$When $n$ is odd, $f(-x)=-kx^n+c\\ne f(x)$ since $k\\ne0$. Hence $n=2,4,6,8,\\ldots$.",
          "solutionDiagrams": []
        },
        "answerSpaceMm": 30
      },
      {
        "id": "v2-7eec2e848-practice-slot-1-s1-q-b",
        "type": "part",
        "label": "b",
        "prompt": "Odd?",
        "layout": "list",
        "columns": null,
        "questionDiagrams": [],
        "children": [],
        "answer": {
          "short": "No values of $n$.",
          "worked": "$$\\begin{align*}f(-x)&=k(-x)^n+c\\\\&=-kx^n+c\\quad\\text{when }n\\text{ is odd}\\\\-f(x)&=-kx^n-c\\end{align*}$$Odd requires $f(-x)=-f(x)$, so $c=-c$, giving $c=0$. This contradicts $c\\ne0$. When $n$ is even, $f$ is even and not odd. Hence no value of $n$ makes $f$ odd.",
          "solutionDiagrams": []
        },
        "answerSpaceMm": 40
      }
    ]
  },
